arXiv:2510.07509v1 [cs.LG] 08 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training 
<br class="ltx_break">under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift
</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tianyu Bell Pan
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Florida Institute for National Security (FINS)
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Applied Artificial Intelligence Group
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Damon L. Woodard
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Florida
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Gainesville, FL, 32611
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{tpan1, dwoodard}@ufl.edu</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper explores a multimodal co-training framework designed to enhance model generalization in situations where labeled data is limited and distribution shifts occur. We thoroughly examine the theoretical foundations of this framework, deriving conditions under which the use of unlabeled data and the promotion of agreement between classifiers for different modalities lead to significant improvements in generalization. We also present a convergence analysis that confirms the effectiveness of iterative co-training in reducing classification errors. In addition, we establish a novel generalization bound that, for the first time in a multimodal co-training context, decomposes and quantifies the distinct advantages gained from leveraging unlabeled multimodal data, promoting inter-view agreement, and maintaining conditional view independence. Our findings highlight the practical benefits of multimodal co-training as a structured approach to developing data-efficient and robust AI systems that can effectively generalize in dynamic, real-world environments. The theoretical foundations are examined in dialogue with, and in advance of, established co-training principles.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id2" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The ability to generalize flexibly and efficiently from limited resources is a key feature of human intelligence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. In contrast, artificial intelligence (AI) models struggle to expand their capabilities when training data and computational resources are scarce, hindering their effective deployment in real-world scenarios that demand learning from sparse samples in unpredictable environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. A primary challenge is the scarcity of labeled data, which is often difficult and costly to obtain, especially in specialized or rapidly evolving fields <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. This reliance on supervised learning reduces the robustness and generalizability of models. Additionally, most of the real-world data is multimodal, originating from diverse sources. While integrating these modalities can enhance understanding and robustness, many existing methods favor single-modality approaches or necessitate substantial labeled multimodal data for effective fusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, AI models face non-stationary inputs and distribution shifts, which can degrade performance as learned patterns from historical data become outdated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Such shifts are not mere academic concerns but practical hurdles, as seen in the evolving nature of misinformation campaigns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> or the performance degradation of even large vision-language models when confronted with discrepancies between training and testing domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Adversarial threats also evolve, introducing further complexity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This paper explores a semi-supervised multimodal co-training framework to tackle the challenge of limited labeled data. By leveraging unlabeled data and ensuring consistency across different modalities, AI systems can enhance their generalization capabilities, even in the face of label scarcity or distribution shifts. We analyze the mathematical foundations underlying this framework, investigating the convergence properties of the multimodal co-training algorithm and deriving a tighter generalization bound that highlights the advantages of using unlabeled data. These theoretical results highlight the potential of unlabeled multimodal data to address the scarcity of labeled data, thereby paving the way for the development of more data-efficient and adaptive AI systems.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Contributions</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The key contributions of this research are:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multimodal co-training:</span> We introduce a theoretically grounded multimodal co-training framework that integrates dual-threshold pseudo-labeling, a customizable agreement loss, and a controlled label-expansion budget. These components are not ad-hoc but are designed to directly optimize factors identified in our theoretical analysis, such as improving pseudo-label reliability to aid convergence and shaping the agreement term <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> in the generalization bound. The framework aims to optimize the analytical mixing coefficient <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and reduce the contraction factor <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Convergence guarantee:</span> We provide a rigorous proof of geometric convergence for the iterative co-training process, demonstrating that classifier error is reduced in expectation under standard co-training assumptions. This analysis clarifies the conditions for stable learning and the role of inter-view information exchange in error reduction, moving beyond heuristic justifications for the efficacy of co-training. Each round of pseudo-label exchange and agreement loss significantly reduces classifier error in expectation, resulting in geometric convergence up to a minimum.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Novel generalization bound:</span> We derive a novel PAC-style generalization risk bound that isolates and quantifies a benefit term (<math id="S1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>). This term captures how the fraction of unlabeled data, the degree of inter-view agreement, and the strength of view on conditional independence collectively tighten the bound. This provides a more interpretable understanding of the value of unlabeled multimodal data in co-training than prior guarantees, surpassing prior co-training and semi-supervised guarantees by offering this decomposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data-efficient learning:</span> We show that increasing the amount of unlabeled data or enhancing view independence and agreement can significantly improve both convergence and generalization. These findings offer practical strategies for leveraging large corpora of unlabeled data and promoting consistency to address label scarcity and distribution shifts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Related Work</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Research on learning with limited annotated data encompasses several areas of study. Few-shot and zero-shot learning (FSL/ZSL) methods aim to generalize from very few or no labeled examples by leveraging auxiliary information, such as semantic attributes or meta-learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. However, these methods often face challenges in high-dimensional or multimodal settings and can be biased when attribute representations are too general and non-discriminative <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Simultaneously, multimodal fusion approaches integrate various types of inputs (e.g., text, images, audio) through early, intermediate, or late fusion strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. However, these approaches typically require large labeled datasets to effectively align and combine different modalities, making them fragile when labeled data is scarce <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, semi-supervised learning (SSL) methods, particularly co-training, utilize unlabeled data by training separate classifiers on conditionally independent "views" and exchanging high-confidence pseudo-labels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. However, existing analyses of SSL mainly focus on single-view scenarios or provide only loose additive guarantees without clearly quantifying how agreement between views reduces risk. Additionally, open-world deployment requires models to effectively generalize under non-stationary data distributions, where distribution shifts can significantly degrade performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. While some approaches utilize drift detection mechanisms to initiate model updates, it is important to understand the robustness of different learning paradigms in response to such shifts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we propose a unified framework that integrates multiple modalities and unlabeled data while ensuring rigorous convergence and risk bounds. Our proposed multimodal co-training framework introduces a novel approach that utilizes at least two different modalities and leverages unlabeled data. Instead of relying solely on the direct integration of representations derived from limited labeled data, the multimodal co-training framework utilizes unlabeled data along with the principle of cross-view consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. This method enhances and regularizes the specific views of individual learners before any final integration, resulting in more refined features.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminary and Problem Formulation</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Notation and Definitions</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the input space and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> be the label space. For binary classification, we consider <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}=\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}=\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}=\{-1,+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}=\{-1,+1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Data instances <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> are assumed to be drawn from a true underlying data distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. This distribution may be non-stationary in open-world scenarios, denoted as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> at a given time <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. We are provided with a set of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> labeled examples, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{labeled}}=\left\{\left(x_{i},y_{i}\right)\right\}_{i=1}^{N_{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>labeled</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{labeled}}=\left\{\left(x_{i},y_{i}\right)\right\}_{i=1}^{N_{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>, drawn from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, we have access to a typically much larger set of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> unlabeled examples, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}=\left\{u_{j}\right\}_{j=1}^{N_{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}=\left\{u_{j}\right\}_{j=1}^{N_{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>, drawn from the marginal distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. A hypothesis (or classifier) is a function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>. The quality of a hypothesis <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is measured by its true risk (generalization error) concerning the distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R(h)=\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim P}[\ell(h(x),y)]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R(h)=\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim P}[\ell(h(x),y)],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a chosen loss function. The empirical risk of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> on the labeled data <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{labeled}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{labeled}</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{R}_{N_{L}}(h)=\frac{1}{N_{L}}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{L}}\ell\left(h\left(x_{i}\right),y_{i}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{R}_{N_{L}}(h)=\frac{1}{N_{L}}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{L}}\ell\left(h\left(x_{i}\right),y_{i}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> be the hypothesis class from which <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen. The complexity of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> can be characterized by measures such as the Vapnik-Chervonenkis (VC) dimension (<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{VC}(\mathcal{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{VC}(\mathcal{H})</annotation></semantics></math>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, or the Rademacher complexity (<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{N}(\mathcal{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{N}(\mathcal{H})</annotation></semantics></math>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>The Multimodal Learning Setting</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a multimodal learning scenario where each input instance <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> comprises representations from at least two distinct modalities. We focus on two views for clarity, but the framework can be extended. For example, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=\left(x^{(1)},x^{(2)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=\left(x^{(1)},x^{(2)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(1)}\in\mathcal{X}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(1)}\in\mathcal{X}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> are features from the first modality (e.g., text features from a document) and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(2)}\in\mathcal{X}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(2)}\in\mathcal{X}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> are features from the second modality (e.g., image features from an associated image). We denote the view-specific feature extractors as <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}:\mathcal{X}^{(1)}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}:\mathcal{X}^{(1)}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(2)}:\mathcal{X}^{(2)}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(2)}:\mathcal{X}^{(2)}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this setting, each modality <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(v)}</annotation></semantics></math> can predict the label <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> with reasonable accuracy. This means that there exist good classifiers <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*(1)}\left(x^{(1)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*(1)}\left(x^{(1)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*(2)}\left(x^{(2)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*(2)}\left(x^{(2)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. However, each view may also be noisy or incomplete; for example, text might be ambiguous, or an image might lack context. The premise is that the complementary information across views can be harnessed for improved learning. The core premise is that while each view is stochastically sufficient, their error patterns are diverse, allowing for mutual correction and enrichment through collaborative learning. This premise is that the complementary information across views can be utilized for improved learning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Problem Formulation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the following two sets of data: (1) a small set of labeled multimodal examples <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{labeled}}=\left\{\left(\left(x_{i}^{(1)},x_{i}^{(2)}\right),y_{i}\right)\right\}_{i=1}^{N_{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>labeled</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{labeled}}=\left\{\left(\left(x_{i}^{(1)},x_{i}^{(2)}\right),y_{i}\right)\right\}_{i=1}^{N_{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>; and (2) a large set of unlabeled multimodal examples <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}=\left\{\left(u_{j}^{(1)},u_{j}^{(2)}\right)\right\}_{j=1}^{N_{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}=\left\{\left(u_{j}^{(1)},u_{j}^{(2)}\right)\right\}_{j=1}^{N_{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The objective is to learn a combined classifier, denoted <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\theta}\left(\left(x^{(1)},x^{(2)}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\theta}\left(\left(x^{(1)},x^{(2)}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, parameterized by <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, that minimizes the true risk <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\left(h_{\theta}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\left(h_{\theta}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> on unseen future data drawn from <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. This classifier <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> typically involves view-specific components <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\theta_{1}}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\theta_{1}}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\theta_{2}}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\theta_{2}}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a mechanism for combining their outputs or enforcing agreement. This paper focuses on the theoretical properties (convergence and generalization) of achieving this objective via a multimodal co-training strategy.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.1 </span>Co-Training Strategy in the Multimodal Context</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The co-training strategy involves two view-specific classifiers, <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> (operating on <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> (operating on <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>). The process begins with initialization, where classifiers <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> are trained on the labeled set <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{labeled}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>labeled</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{labeled}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This initial training sets the foundation for the subsequent iterative refinement steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the next phase, labeled examples are further enhanced through iterative pseudo-labeling and retraining. Classifier <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> predicts labels for examples in the unlabeled set <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. From these predictions, a confident subset <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{\text{pseudo}}^{(1)}\subset\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mtext>pseudo</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{\text{pseudo}}^{(1)}\subset\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is selected, and these examples are assigned pseudo-labels <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The pseudo-labeled instances <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\left(u_{j},\hat{y}_{j}^{(1)}\right)\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\left(u_{j},\hat{y}_{j}^{(1)}\right)\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> are then utilized to augment the training set for <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, classifier <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> performs the same for <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> by pseudo-labeling a confident subset <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{\text{pseudo}}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mtext>pseudo</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{\text{pseudo}}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Both <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> are subsequently retrained using the labeled set <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{labeled}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>labeled</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{labeled}}</annotation></semantics></math> along with their respective augmented pseudo-labeled datasets. This process is repeated for several rounds until a convergence criterion is satisfied.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To further leverage the unlabeled data and enforce consistency between the two classifiers, an agreement loss term (<math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{agree}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>agree</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{agree}}</annotation></semantics></math>) can be incorporated. This term penalizes discrepancies between the predictions of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> on the same examples <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a given unlabeled instance <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=\left(u^{(1)},u^{(2)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=\left(u^{(1)},u^{(2)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{(1)}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{(1)}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{(2)}(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{(2)}(u)</annotation></semantics></math> represent the probabilistic outputs from <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the agreement loss can be formulated as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{agree}}=\mathbb{E}_{u\sim\mathcal{U}}D," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>agree</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{agree}}=\mathbb{E}_{u\sim\mathcal{U}}D,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is a divergence or distance measure, such as the squared difference <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(p^{(1)}-p^{(2)}\right)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(p^{(1)}-p^{(2)}\right)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or cross-entropy if one model’s output serves as a target for the other. This loss term is typically integrated with the supervised loss during training, expressed as <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{sup}}+\lambda_{\text{agree}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{agree}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>sup</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>agree</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>agree</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{sup}}+\lambda_{\text{agree}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{agree}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{sup}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>sup</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{sup}}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the empirical risk on the labeled set <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{labeled}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>labeled</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{labeled}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{agree}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>agree</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{agree}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a weighting factor that balances the two loss components. Additionally, mechanisms are implemented to regulate the rate of label expansion, which helps minimize the risk of error propagation throughout the training process.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Proposed Algorithm</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.4 Proposed Algorithm ‣ 2 Preliminary and Problem Formulation ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is hence proposed. This algorithm trains separate classifiers on each view, encouraging mutual supervision through pseudo-label exchange and cross-view consistency. The key insight is that each classifier can help label examples for the other when confident, and both classifiers are encouraged to agree on predictions for unlabeled data.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Multimodal Co<span id="alg1.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>Training</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
              
              
             Labeled set <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\{((x_{i}^{(1)},x_{i}^{(2)}),y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N_{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\{((x_{i}^{(1)},x_{i}^{(2)}),y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N_{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>; Unlabeled set <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}=\{(u_{j}^{(1)},u_{j}^{(2)})\}_{j=1}^{N_{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}=\{(u_{j}^{(1)},u_{j}^{(2)})\}_{j=1}^{N_{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>; Iterations <math id="alg1.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>; thresholds <math id="alg1.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\mathrm{pseudo}},\tau_{\mathrm{agree}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>pseudo</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>agree</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\mathrm{pseudo}},\tau_{\mathrm{agree}}</annotation></semantics></math>; pseudo<span id="alg1.l0.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>label count <math id="alg1.l0.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\mathrm{pseudo}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>pseudo</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\mathrm{pseudo}}</annotation></semantics></math>; agreement weight <math id="alg1.l0.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{agree}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>agree</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{agree}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l0a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
              
              
             Trained models <math id="alg1.l0a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}_{\theta_{1}},\,h^{(2)}_{\theta_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}_{\theta_{1}},\,h^{(2)}_{\theta_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
              
              
             Initialize <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}_{\theta_{1}},h^{(2)}_{\theta_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}_{\theta_{1}},h^{(2)}_{\theta_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>; set <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{\text{pool}}\leftarrow\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mtext>pool</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{\text{pool}}\leftarrow\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{1},\mathcal{P}_{2}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{1},\mathcal{P}_{2}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for all</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u^{(1)},u^{(2)})\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{pool}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>pool</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u^{(1)},u^{(2)})\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{pool}}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
              
              
                <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{u}^{(v)},c_{u}^{(v)})\leftarrow\mathrm{Predict}(h^{(v)}_{\theta_{v}},u^{(v)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>Predict</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{u}^{(v)},c_{u}^{(v)})\leftarrow\mathrm{Predict}(h^{(v)}_{\theta_{v}},u^{(v)})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
              
              
                <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{u}^{(v)}&gt;\tau_{\mathrm{pseudo}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>pseudo</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{u}^{(v)}&gt;\tau_{\mathrm{pseudo}}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
              
              
                 <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{u}^{(v)}\leftarrow\mathrm{PseudoLabel}(p_{u}^{(v)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>PseudoLabel</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{u}^{(v)}\leftarrow\mathrm{PseudoLabel}(p_{u}^{(v)})</annotation></semantics></math>; add <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((u^{(1)},u^{(2)}),\hat{y}_{u}^{(v)},c_{u}^{(v)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((u^{(1)},u^{(2)}),\hat{y}_{u}^{(v)},c_{u}^{(v)})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{3-v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{3-v}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
