              <span id="alg1.l14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">15:</span>
              
              
               Freeze <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(3-v)}_{\theta_{3-v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(3-v)}_{\theta_{3-v}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">16:</span>
              
              
               Update <math id="alg1.l16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{v}\leftarrow\arg\min_{\theta}\Bigl[\sum\mathcal{L}_{\sup}+\lambda_{\mathrm{agree}}\sum\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{agree}}\Bigr]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>sup</mo></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>agree</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>agree</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{v}\leftarrow\arg\min_{\theta}\Bigl[\sum\mathcal{L}_{\sup}+\lambda_{\mathrm{agree}}\sum\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{agree}}\Bigr]</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">17:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l17.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">18:</span>
              
              
              Remove all <math id="alg1.l18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{pseudo}}^{(1)}\cup\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{pseudo}}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>pseudo</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>pseudo</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{pseudo}}^{(1)}\cup\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{pseudo}}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="alg1.l18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{pool}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>pool</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{pool}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">19:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{pseudo}}^{(1)}\cup\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{pseudo}}^{(2)}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>pseudo</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∪</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>pseudo</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{pseudo}}^{(1)}\cup\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{pseudo}}^{(2)}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">20:</span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">break</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l21" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">21:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l22" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">22:</span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l22.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l23" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">23:</span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}_{\theta_{1}},\,h^{(2)}_{\theta_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}_{\theta_{1}},\,h^{(2)}_{\theta_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Main Results</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents the main results concerning the convergence and generalization of this proposed framework.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Assumptions</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze the main framework, three assumptions are defined:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Multi-View Structure and Sufficiency)</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The learning problem involves at least two views of the data, denoted <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to different modalities. We assume that:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">View sufficiency:</span><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Each view </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(v)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, for </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, is individually sufficient for classification. Formally, for each view </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, there exists a hypothesis </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*(v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*(v)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> within the hypothesis space </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{(v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{(v)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that its true risk </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\left(h^{*(v)}\right)\leq\epsilon_{\text{suff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">suff</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\left(h^{*(v)}\right)\leq\epsilon_{\text{suff}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, where </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{suff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">suff</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{suff}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a small positive constant.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Conditional independence of views:</span><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Given the true label </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the features </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are conditionally independent. That is, </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\left(x^{(1)},x^{(2)}|y\right)=P\left(x^{(1)}|y\right)P\left(x^{(2)}|y\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\left(x^{(1)},x^{(2)}|y\right)=P\left(x^{(1)}|y\right)P\left(x^{(2)}|y\right)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This assumption is foundational to the co-training paradigm. View sufficiency ensures that each modality-specific classifier has a learnable target and can, in principle, make accurate predictions. Conditional independence is critical for the efficacy of the co-training process. It implies that the errors made by one view are not systematically correlated with the errors of the other, given the true class.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Classifier Quality and Pseudo-Label Reliability)</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The co-training process effectively bootstraps performance based on initial classifier quality and the reliability of the information exchanged between views. We assume that:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Initial classifier quality:</span><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> The initial classifiers </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(v)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, trained on the available labeled data </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, have an error rate </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{v}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> on the unlabeled data distribution that is better than random change (i.e., </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{v}&lt;\frac{1}{2}-\delta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{v}&lt;\frac{1}{2}-\delta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for some small </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Pseudo-label reliability:</span><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> During the co-training process, pseudo-labels </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}^{(v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}^{(v)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> generated by a classifier </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(v)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(v)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for confidently predicted unlabeled instances </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are correct with high probability.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This assumption is that initial classifiers possess a baseline level of competence (i.e., better-than-random performance) and that the subsequent pseudo-labeling process maintains high reliability. The initial classifier quality is important because co-training is a bootstrapping mechanism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Model Learnability and Loss Properties)</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">To ensure that the learning process is well-posed and that generalization can be theoretically analyzed, we make standard assumptions regarding the model and loss function:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Bounded hypothesis complexity:</span><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> The hypothesis class </span><math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has bounded complexity. This can be characterized by a finite VC-dimension, </span><math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{VC}(\mathcal{H})\leq d_{\text{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">eff</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{VC}(\mathcal{H})\leq d_{\text{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, or by Rademacher complexity </span><math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{N}(\mathcal{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{N}(\mathcal{H})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> that grows sub-linearly with </span><math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Loss function properties:</span><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> The loss function </span><math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> used for training is bounded.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This assumption is a standard in statistical learning theory, ensuring the learning problem is well-posed and analyzable. Bounded hypothesis complexity prevents the models from becoming overly complex and overfitting the training data, including the initial labeled set and the incrementally added pseudo-labeled examples. This control over complexity is fundamental for deriving generalization bounds that guarantee performance on unseen data. Properties of the loss function ensure stability in risk calculations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Convergence of Multimodal Co-Training</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before presenting the convergence theorem, we introduce Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4 (Expected Improvement via Pseudo-Labeling) ‣ 3.2 Convergence of Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, which describes the expected behavior of a classifier trained on pseudo-labels provided by a slightly more accurate view.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Expected Improvement via Pseudo-Labeling)</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> be classifiers on two conditionally independent views with true error rates <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, where</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\epsilon^{(2)}&lt;\min\bigl\{\epsilon^{(1)},\tfrac{1}{2}\bigr\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\epsilon^{(2)}&lt;\min\bigl\{\epsilon^{(1)},\tfrac{1}{2}\bigr\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is retrained on a sufficiently large set of pseudo-labels produced by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and that these pseudo-labels are reliable under the conditional independence assumption. Then, there exists <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\bigl[\epsilon^{\prime(1)}\bigr]\;\leq\;(1-\alpha)\,\epsilon^{(1)}\;+\;\alpha\,\epsilon^{(2)}\;&lt;\;\epsilon^{(1)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">&lt;</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\bigl[\epsilon^{\prime(1)}\bigr]\;\leq\;(1-\alpha)\,\epsilon^{(1)}\;+\;\alpha\,\epsilon^{(2)}\;&lt;\;\epsilon^{(1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This lemma shows that a less accurate classifier can improve by learning from a more accurate one, given that the views are conditionally independent. Conditional independence prevents the pseudo-labels from <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> from being systematically biased like <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>’s errors. If <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> is more accurate and its pseudo-labels are mostly correct, <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> can use these examples to correct its mistakes. The view sufficiency condition is important. A small number of pseudo-labels may not be sufficient to overcome existing biases, but a large set can drive effective learning. This single-step improvement is key to the iterative convergence of the co-training algorithm. Lastly, the expectation <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> captures the randomness in selecting unlabeled data and the noise in the pseudo-labels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Building on Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4 (Expected Improvement via Pseudo-Labeling) ‣ 3.2 Convergence of Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, we demonstrate that the errors of the view-specific classifiers are expected to decrease under certain conditions. The first theorem focuses on the convergence of the iterative co-training process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Co-Training Convergence)</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1,k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1,k)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(2,k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(2,k)}</annotation></semantics></math> be the classifiers on views 1 and 2 after <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> rounds of co-training, with true error rates <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{(1,k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{(1,k)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{(2,k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{(2,k)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under Assumptions <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 3.1 (Multi-View Structure and Sufficiency) ‣ 3.1 Assumptions ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 3.2 (Classifier Quality and Pseudo-Label Reliability) ‣ 3.1 Assumptions ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, there exists</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha\in(0,1),\;\lambda=1-\alpha\in(0,1),\;c_{\min}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha\in(0,1),\;\lambda=1-\alpha\in(0,1),\;c_{\min}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">such that, with high probability for each <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\neq i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\neq i</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\bigl[\epsilon^{(i,k+1)}\bigr]\leq(1-\alpha)\,\epsilon^{(i,k)}+\alpha\,\epsilon^{(j,k)}+c_{\min}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\bigl[\epsilon^{(i,k+1)}\bigr]\leq(1-\alpha)\,\epsilon^{(i,k)}+\alpha\,\epsilon^{(j,k)}+c_{\min},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and hence</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{i=1,2}\mathbb{E}\bigl[\epsilon^{(i,k+1)}\bigr]\leq\lambda\max_{i=1,2}\epsilon^{(i,k)}+c_{\min}&lt;\max_{i=1,2}\epsilon^{(i,k)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{i=1,2}\mathbb{E}\bigl[\epsilon^{(i,k+1)}\bigr]\leq\lambda\max_{i=1,2}\epsilon^{(i,k)}+c_{\min}&lt;\max_{i=1,2}\epsilon^{(i,k)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> accounts for irreducible noise or finite-data effects, and in the ideal infinite-data limit, it approaches the Bayes error of the combined views.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Co-Training Convergence) ‣ 3.2 Convergence of Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> extends this single-step improvement to an iterative process, showing geometric convergence of the maximum error across views towards a residual error floor (<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math>). The contraction factor (<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>) dictates the speed of convergence: a smaller <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and larger <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> lead to faster error reduction and more effective learning from the peer view, respectively. <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the limit of what can be learned through this process, influenced by factors like inherent noise, the Bayes error of the combined views, and finite sample effects.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.07509v1/error_contract.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/300;" width="381" height="300" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Error-Contraction Surface from Simulation. It illustrates how the maximum error <math id="S3.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> evolves over different rounds <math id="S3.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and contraction factors <math id="S3.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This theorem also shows that both view-specific classifiers improve iteratively and converge to a small residual error under ideal co-training assumptions. At each round <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4 (Expected Improvement via Pseudo-Labeling) ‣ 3.2 Convergence of Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> ensures that retraining classifier <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(i,k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(i,k)}</annotation></semantics></math> on sufficiently many pseudo-labels from the other view <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(j,k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(j,k)}</annotation></semantics></math> yields an expected error (Eq.(7)) with mixing weight <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and a nonnegative floor <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> accounting for irreducible noise or finite-data effects. Whenever <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{(j,k)}&lt;\epsilon^{(i,k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{(j,k)}&lt;\epsilon^{(i,k)}</annotation></semantics></math>, this bound guarantees <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\epsilon^{(i,k+1)}\right]&lt;\epsilon^{(i,k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\epsilon^{(i,k+1)}\right]&lt;\epsilon^{(i,k)}</annotation></semantics></math>, establishing an improvement in each one-step update. By applying the same bound to both views and taking the maximum error into account, Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Co-Training Convergence) ‣ 3.2 Convergence of Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> demonstrates a geometric contraction up to the residual <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{min}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq.(8)). This type of convergence behavior is a hallmark of successful bootstrapping algorithms. Similar analyses exist for self-training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> and other semi-supervised methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>; however, our result is specific to the multimodal co-training dynamics and the interplay between two classifiers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.2 Convergence of Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>’s surface shows a rapid decay of error, transitioning from warm colors to cool colors, and approaches an asymptote at the irreducible error floor. A higher degree of independence or a larger accuracy gap results in a smaller <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to faster convergence. As more representative unlabeled data is incorporated, the value of <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{min}</annotation></semantics></math> decreases, bringing the error floor closer to the Bayes optimum.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond convergence, it is important to understand how well the learned models will generalize to unseen data. Before the main theorem, we introduce a proposition that clarifies the nature of the benefit derived from unlabeled data in this co-training context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Benefit of Unlabeled Data)</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In multimodal co-training over <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> labeled and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> unlabeled examples, the "benefit" term</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Gamma=\Gamma\Bigl(\tfrac{N_{U}}{N_{L}+N_{U}},d(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}),\mathrm{indep}\Bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>indep</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Gamma=\Gamma\Bigl(\tfrac{N_{U}}{N_{L}+N_{U}},d(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}),\mathrm{indep}\Bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">satisfies <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\partial\Gamma}{\partial\bigl(N_{U}/(N_{L}+N_{U})\bigr)}&gt;0,\;\frac{\partial\Gamma}{\partial\,d(h^{(1)},h^{(2)})}&lt;0,\;\frac{\partial\Gamma}{\partial\,\mathrm{indep}}&gt;0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">∂</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">∂</mo><mi>indep</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\partial\Gamma}{\partial\bigl(N_{U}/(N_{L}+N_{U})\bigr)}&gt;0,\;\frac{\partial\Gamma}{\partial\,d(h^{(1)},h^{(2)})}&lt;0,\;\frac{\partial\Gamma}{\partial\,\mathrm{indep}}&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Equivalently:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> grows with the fraction of unlabeled data </span><math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{N_{U}}{N_{L}+N_{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{N_{U}}{N_{L}+N_{U}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> increases as the classifiers’ disagreement </span><math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(h^{(1)},h^{(2)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(h^{(1)},h^{(2)})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> decreases.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> increases with stronger conditional independence of the two views.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 3.6 (Benefit of Unlabeled Data) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a> introduces the benefit term <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, which is central to our novel generalization bound. It establishes that <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is not merely an abstract quantity but is directly and positively influenced by the proportion of unlabeled data utilized, the degree of agreement (low <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>) between the view-specific classifiers on this unlabeled data, and the strength of conditional independence between the views. This proposition sets the stage for understanding how co-training translates unlabeled data and inter-view consistency into improved generalization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The proposition illustrates that unlabeled data becomes valuable only when co-training leverages the agreement between two independent views. When the classifiers <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math> seldom disagree on the majority of unlabeled examples, their matching labels serve as a high-confidence signal. This helps to regularize the hypothesis space and reduces the risk of overfitting to the small labeled dataset. The disagreement rate <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> serves as a measure of view alignment: a low <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that the models capture compatible structures, which enhances <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the value that co-training derives from unlabeled data through multiview consistency, as determined by theoretical generalization bounds that improve as the level of disagreement among the unlabeled data decreases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (Generalization Bound)</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\theta}\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\theta}\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> be the model returned by multimodal co-training on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> labeled and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> unlabeled samples, where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> has effective complexity <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under Assumptions <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 3.1 (Multi-View Structure and Sufficiency) ‣ 3.1 Assumptions ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>-<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Assumption 3.3 (Model Learnability and Loss Properties) ‣ 3.1 Assumptions ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, for any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, with probability at least <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R(h_{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R(h_{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\underbrace{\hat{R}_{N_{L}}(h_{\theta})}_{\text{empirical risk}}+C_{1}\sqrt{\frac{d_{\mathrm{eff}}\ln(N_{L}/d_{\mathrm{eff}})+\ln(1/\delta)}{N_{L}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">empirical risk</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\underbrace{\hat{R}_{N_{L}}(h_{\theta})}_{\text{empirical risk}}+C_{1}\sqrt{\frac{d_{\mathrm{eff}}\ln(N_{L}/d_{\mathrm{eff}})+\ln(1/\delta)}{N_{L}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\underbrace{\Gamma\Bigl(\tfrac{N_{U}}{N_{L}+N_{U}},\mathrm{agreement},\mathrm{indep}\Bigr)}_{\begin{subarray}{c}\text{unlabeled benefit}\end{subarray}}+C_{2}\sqrt{\frac{\ln(1/\delta)}{N_{L}+N_{U}}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi>agreement</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>indep</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtable><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">unlabeled benefit</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\underbrace{\Gamma\Bigl(\tfrac{N_{U}}{N_{L}+N_{U}},\mathrm{agreement},\mathrm{indep}\Bigr)}_{\begin{subarray}{c}\text{unlabeled benefit}\end{subarray}}+C_{2}\sqrt{\frac{\ln(1/\delta)}{N_{L}+N_{U}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(h_{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(h_{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> is the true risk, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{N_{L}}(h_{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{N_{L}}(h_{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> the empirical risk on labeled data, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> grows with the unlabeled fraction, classifier agreement, and view independence, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are universal constants.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 3.7 (Generalization Bound) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a> presents our main result in generalization theory: a PAC-style bound for multimodal co-training. Its primary novelty lies in the subtractive unlabeled benefit term (<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\Gamma(\dots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\Gamma(\dots)</annotation></semantics></math>), While prior co-training bounds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> established that co-training can reduce error, this bound offers a more granular decomposition, attributing a quantifiable benefit to the interplay of unlabeled data volume, inter-view agreement, and view independence. This contrasts with bounds where such benefits might be implicitly absorbed into complexity terms or empirical risk calculations. The term <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{R}_{N_{L}}\left(h_{\theta}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{R}_{N_{L}}\left(h_{\theta}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard empirical risk on <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> labeled samples, while <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}\sqrt{\frac{d_{\text{eff}}\ln\left(N_{L}/d_{\text{eff}}\right)+\ln(1/\delta)}{N_{L}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>eff</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>eff</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}\sqrt{\frac{d_{\text{eff}}\ln\left(N_{L}/d_{\text{eff}}\right)+\ln(1/\delta)}{N_{L}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a standard complexity penalty for supervised learning. The core innovation is <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\Gamma(\dots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\Gamma(\dots)</annotation></semantics></math>, a negative term that reduces the overall error bound; its magnitude, detailed in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 3.6 (Benefit of Unlabeled Data) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>, grows with more unlabeled data, better agreement, and stronger independence, directly modeling the benefit co-training derives from unlabeled data under favorable view conditions. The final term, <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}\sqrt{\frac{\ln(1/\delta)}{N_{L}+N_{U}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}\sqrt{\frac{\ln(1/\delta)}{N_{L}+N_{U}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, can be interpreted as a smaller penalty related to the confidence in estimating agreement from the total pool of <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}+N_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}+N_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> data, which also diminishes as <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> grows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advancements in SSL generalization theory have explored various avenues, including information-theoretic bounds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, bounds for SSL under distribution shifts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, and bounds accounting for multimodal interactions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. More recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> has proposed universal generalization bounds for reciprocal learning that aim to minimize distributional assumptions by focusing on verifiable algorithmic conditions. Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 3.7 (Generalization Bound) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a> makes a unique contribution by providing a PAC-style analysis that disentangles the specific contributions of unlabeled data characteristics within the co-training paradigm, offering a transparent analytical tool for understanding these interactions. It builds upon previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> by isolating and quantifying the benefits derived from unlabeled data as a distinct, subtractive term within the generalization bound, which is significant in providing a clear, interpretable, and structured framework for understanding generalization in multimodal co-training. Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents a comparative analysis of this generalization bound with other relevant paradigms.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Comparative Analysis of Generalization Bounds in Co-Training and Related Semi-Supervised Learning Paradigms</figcaption>
