<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>More Related Work</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Few-Shot and Zero-Shot Learning (FSL/ZSL)</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">FSL and ZSL aim to facilitate learning with very few or no labeled examples per class. While these methods are promising in limited-data scenarios, they encounter significant challenges in high-dimensional or multimodal settings. For instance, ZSL often struggles to generalize with domain-specific data, leading to lower accuracy and potential bias. FSL, on the other hand, can be sensitive to the selection of support examples or the design of prompts, impacting its reliability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Challenges of multimodal scenarios, such as few-shot action recognition using video data, include effectively extracting spatiotemporal features and learning generalizable representations from limited sample sizes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. High intra-class variance, common in real-world data, poses particular challenges and often results in overfitting when sample sizes are scarce. Additionally, traditional ZSL typically assumes a single semantic attribute vector per class, which is inadequate for categories with diverse appearances or multiple distinct modes. For example, different plumages of a bird species may not be well represented by a “one-size-fits-all" attribute vector, leading to too general and non-discriminative attributes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed multimodal co-training framework presents a novel approach that utilizes at least two modalities and leverages unlabeled data. This design accommodates various aspects of the data. For example, if one view captures textual descriptions and another captures visual appearances, co-training can address the limitations of relying on a “single attribute vector." This method provides richer representations, enabling more effective handling of intra-class variance than many FSL or ZSL methods, especially when a wealth of unlabeled multimodal data is available.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Multimodal Learning</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multimodal learning integrates information from various data types, such as text, images, and audio <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Many supervised approaches are “fusion-heavy," meaning they combine features or decisions from different modalities at different stages, including early, intermediate, or late fusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. Although these methods are effective with plenty of labeled data, they usually require large amounts of labeled multimodal examples to learn valuable cross-modal interactions and create robust fused representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Several challenges arise in this process, such as ensuring that the features are semantically aligned before fusion, managing the variability and reliability differences between modalities, handling high-dimensional data, and achieving optimal fusion of views. When labeled data is limited, the features learned for each modality can be noisy or unrepresentative, resulting in suboptimal fusion outcomes.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed multimodal co-training framework introduces a unique approach to data processing. Instead of solely relying on the direct integration of representations obtained from limited labeled data, it utilizes unlabeled data and the principle of cross-view consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. This method regularizes and enhances individual learners’ specific views before any final integration takes place, resulting in more refined features. The agreement mechanism is an implicit form of learned integration driven by unlabeled data. This positions co-training as a more efficient alternative or an essential complement to supervised integration.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Semi-supervised learning (SSL) and Co-Training</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SSL methods utilize both labeled and unlabeled data for training. The co-training paradigm, introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, is especially effective in multimodal settings. It is based on two main assumptions: (1) multiple "views" or modalities are independently sufficient for classification, and (2) these views are conditionally independent given the class label. Classifiers trained on their respective views iteratively assign pseudo-labels to confident examples from the unlabeled data, allowing them to train each other. This approach enables the models to effectively "teach" one another, thereby expanding the training set by leveraging information from unlabeled data.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While the foundational principles of co-training are well-known, there remains a lack of rigorous analyses, particularly concerning convergence and generalization bounds in multimodal semi-supervised learning. This gap is especially evident when examining aspects such as the adequacy of different views, conditional independence, and the role of unlabeled data in refining these bounds. This paper aims to deepen the theoretical understanding of these concepts by providing a more robust mathematical framework for applying co-training in complex, multimodal, and data-scarce settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Open-World Generalization and Distribution Shift</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Open-world deployment requires models to effectively generalize under non-stationary data distributions, where concept drift or distribution shift can significantly degrade performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. For instance, misinformation campaigns continuously evolve in narratives and styles, which can cause static models to struggle. While some approaches utilize drift detection mechanisms to initiate model updates, it is important to understand the robustness of different learning paradigms in response to such shifts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. Recent Test-Time Adaptation (TTA) techniques aim to adjust models to ongoing shifts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, but the challenge remains significant, with even large foundation models sometimes faltering under such conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The co-training mechanism in the proposed framework allows for a degree of passive adaptation to evolving data streams. This theoretical framework examines the generalization bounds that incorporate agreement on unlabeled data, providing insights into how co-training can maintain performance. If unlabeled data is drawn from the current, potentially shifted distribution, enforcing consistency on this data can help the model stay aligned with changes. This paper offers a foundational understanding of co-training behavior in non-stationary environments, which is important for analyzing more complex adaptive systems. A key consideration is the robustness of the derived bounds in the face of variations introduced by non-stationarity.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Proof</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Proof of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4 (Expected Improvement via Pseudo-Labeling) ‣ 3.2 Convergence of Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a></h3>

<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof B.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> denote the true data distribution over <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathrm{pl}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>pl</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{pl}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the distribution over ( <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y</annotation></semantics></math> ) obtained by drawing <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\sim D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\sim D</annotation></semantics></math> and assigning it the pseudo-label <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{y}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{y}=</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(2)}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(2)}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. For any mixing weight <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, define the mixture</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{mix }}=(1-\alpha)D+\alpha D_{\mathrm{pl}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">mix </mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>pl</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{mix }}=(1-\alpha)D+\alpha D_{\mathrm{pl}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumptions <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 3.1 (Multi-View Structure and Sufficiency) ‣ 3.1 Assumptions ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 3.2 (Classifier Quality and Pseudo-Label Reliability) ‣ 3.1 Assumptions ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>(ii), the Bayes error on <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathrm{pl}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>pl</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{pl}}</annotation></semantics></math> equals <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\backslash\mathrm{eps}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><msup><mi>eps</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\backslash\mathrm{eps}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the Bayes risk on the mixture satisfies</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{D_{\text{mix }}}^{*}\leq(1-\alpha)\epsilon^{(1)}+\alpha\epsilon^{(2)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">mix </mtext></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{D_{\text{mix }}}^{*}\leq(1-\alpha)\epsilon^{(1)}+\alpha\epsilon^{(2)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Retraining <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. samples from <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{mix}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">mix</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{mix}}</annotation></semantics></math> produces a classifier whose expected error satisfies the standard excess-risk decomposition</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\epsilon^{\prime(1)}\right]\leq R_{D_{\operatorname{mix}}}^{*}+\xi_{N}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>mix</mi></msub><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\epsilon^{\prime(1)}\right]\leq R_{D_{\operatorname{mix}}}^{*}+\xi_{N},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{N}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{N}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math> by uniform convergence (e.g., VC or Rademacher complexity bounds). Combining the above,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\epsilon^{\prime(1)}\right]\leq(1-\alpha)\epsilon^{(1)}+\alpha\epsilon^{(2)}+\xi_{N}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\epsilon^{\prime(1)}\right]\leq(1-\alpha)\epsilon^{(1)}+\alpha\epsilon^{(2)}+\xi_{N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Since <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{(2)}&lt;\epsilon^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{(2)}&lt;\epsilon^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can choose <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> large enough that
<math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{N}\leq\frac{\alpha}{2}\left(\epsilon^{(1)}-\epsilon^{(2)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{N}\leq\frac{\alpha}{2}\left(\epsilon^{(1)}-\epsilon^{(2)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\alpha)\epsilon^{(1)}+\alpha\epsilon^{(2)}+\xi_{N}&lt;(1-\alpha)\epsilon^{(1)}+\alpha\epsilon^{(2)}+\frac{\alpha}{2}\left(\epsilon^{(1)}-\epsilon^{(2)}\right)=\epsilon^{(1)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\alpha)\epsilon^{(1)}+\alpha\epsilon^{(2)}+\xi_{N}&lt;(1-\alpha)\epsilon^{(1)}+\alpha\epsilon^{(2)}+\frac{\alpha}{2}\left(\epsilon^{(1)}-\epsilon^{(2)}\right)=\epsilon^{(1)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">establishing
<math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\epsilon^{\prime(1)}\right]&lt;\epsilon^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\epsilon^{\prime(1)}\right]&lt;\epsilon^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>. This completes the proof of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4 (Expected Improvement via Pseudo-Labeling) ‣ 3.2 Convergence of Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Co-Training Convergence) ‣ 3.2 Convergence of Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a></h3>

<div id="A2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof B.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4 (Expected Improvement via Pseudo-Labeling) ‣ 3.2 Convergence of Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>, when classifier <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(i,k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(i,k)}</annotation></semantics></math> is retrained on a sufficiently large, high-confidence pseudo-labeled set provided by <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(j,k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(j,k)}</annotation></semantics></math>, its expected error satisfies</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\epsilon^{(i,k+1)}\right]\leq(1-\alpha)\epsilon^{(i,k)}+\alpha\epsilon^{(j,k)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\epsilon^{(i,k+1)}\right]\leq(1-\alpha)\epsilon^{(i,k)}+\alpha\epsilon^{(j,k)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for some mixing weight <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> determined by the fraction and reliability of pseudo-labels.
In practice, an irreducible floor <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\min}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\min}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> accounts for Bayes-error and finite-sample effects, yielding exactly inequality (7).
Define the worst-case error at round <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> as</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\max}^{(k)}=\max\left\{\epsilon^{(1,k)},\epsilon^{(2,k)}\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\max}^{(k)}=\max\left\{\epsilon^{(1,k)},\epsilon^{(2,k)}\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Applying (7) for both <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=2</annotation></semantics></math> and then taking the maximum gives</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{i}\mathbb{E}\left[\epsilon^{(i,k+1)}\right]\leq\max_{i}\left[(1-\alpha)\epsilon^{(i,k)}+\alpha\epsilon^{(j,k)}+c_{\min}\right]\leq(1-\alpha)\epsilon_{\max}^{(k)}+\alpha\epsilon_{\max}^{(k)}+c_{\min}=\epsilon_{\max}^{(k)}+c_{\min}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>i</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>i</mi></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{i}\mathbb{E}\left[\epsilon^{(i,k+1)}\right]\leq\max_{i}\left[(1-\alpha)\epsilon^{(i,k)}+\alpha\epsilon^{(j,k)}+c_{\min}\right]\leq(1-\alpha)\epsilon_{\max}^{(k)}+\alpha\epsilon_{\max}^{(k)}+c_{\min}=\epsilon_{\max}^{(k)}+c_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Setting <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> yields the geometric-contraction form</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{i}\mathbb{E}\left[\epsilon^{(i,k+1)}\right]\leq\lambda\epsilon_{\max}^{(k)}+c_{\min}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>i</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{i}\mathbb{E}\left[\epsilon^{(i,k+1)}\right]\leq\lambda\epsilon_{\max}^{(k)}+c_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">as claimed. Whenever <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{(j,k)}&lt;\epsilon^{(i,k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{(j,k)}&lt;\epsilon^{(i,k)}</annotation></semantics></math> (ensured by Assumption <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 3.2 (Classifier Quality and Pseudo-Label Reliability) ‣ 3.1 Assumptions ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>(i)), each convex combination <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\alpha)\epsilon^{(i,k)}+\alpha\epsilon^{(j,k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\alpha)\epsilon^{(i,k)}+\alpha\epsilon^{(j,k)}</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly less than <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{(i,k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{(i,k)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, if</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\min}&lt;(1-\lambda)\epsilon_{\max}^{(k)}=\alpha\epsilon_{\max}^{(k)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\min}&lt;(1-\lambda)\epsilon_{\max}^{(k)}=\alpha\epsilon_{\max}^{(k)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\max_{i=1,2}\epsilon^{(i,k)}+c_{\min}&lt;\max_{i=1,2}\epsilon^{(i,k)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\max_{i=1,2}\epsilon^{(i,k)}+c_{\min}&lt;\max_{i=1,2}\epsilon^{(i,k)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which establishes a strict geometric decrease in the worst-case error each round. Together, these show that under the standard co-training assumptions, the maximum of the two view-errors shrinks geometrically toward the irreducible floor <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math>. This completes the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 3.5 (Co-Training Convergence) ‣ 3.2 Convergence of Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Proof of Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 3.6 (Benefit of Unlabeled Data) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a></h3>

<div id="A2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof B.3</span></span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">By construction (see the mixture-error argument in Proof <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Proof B.1 ‣ B.1 Proof of Lemma ‣ Appendix B Proof ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>), enforcing agreement on pseudo-labels can only decrease the Bayes risk on the retraining mixture compared to training purely on labeled data. Hence, the subtractive term <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> in the generalization bound is always nonnegative.
Let</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta=\frac{N_{U}}{N_{L}+N_{U}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta=\frac{N_{U}}{N_{L}+N_{U}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">be the weight on the pseudo-labeled component in the retraining mixture <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{mix }}=(1-\eta)D+\eta D_{\text{pl }}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">mix </mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">pl </mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{mix }}=(1-\eta)D+\eta D_{\text{pl }}</annotation></semantics></math>. A larger <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> places more emphasis on the pseudo-labels, which yields strictly greater error reduction under Assumptions <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Assumption 3.1 (Multi-View Structure and Sufficiency) ‣ 3.1 Assumptions ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 3.2 (Classifier Quality and Pseudo-Label Reliability) ‣ 3.1 Assumptions ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. Thus,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\Gamma}{\partial\eta}\;&gt;\;0\;\text{(Monotonicity in the unlabeled fraction)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em">&gt;</mo><mrow><mn> 0</mn><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">(Monotonicity in the unlabeled fraction)</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\Gamma}{\partial\eta}\;&gt;\;0\;\text{(Monotonicity in the unlabeled fraction)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Define</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right)=\operatorname{Pr}_{x\sim P}\left[h^{(1)}(x)\neq h^{(2)}(x)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Pr</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right)=\operatorname{Pr}_{x\sim P}\left[h^{(1)}(x)\neq h^{(2)}(x)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">on the unlabeled pool. A higher disagreement indicates that the two views disagree more frequently on pseudolabels, thereby reducing their joint reliability. Consequently, the co-training benefit <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> decreases as <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> increases. Thus,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\Gamma}{\partial\,d\bigl(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\bigr)}\;&lt;\;0\;\text{(Monotonicity in view disagreement)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">∂</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mn> 0</mn><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">(Monotonicity in view disagreement)</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\Gamma}{\partial\,d\bigl(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\bigr)}\;&lt;\;0\;\text{(Monotonicity in view disagreement)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Finally, stronger conditional-independence, i.e. a tighter factorization <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P\left(x^{(1)},x^{(2)}\mid y\right)=P\left(x^{(1)}\mid\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">∣</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\left(x^{(1)},x^{(2)}\mid y\right)=P\left(x^{(1)}\mid\right.</annotation></semantics></math> y) <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\left(x^{(2)}\mid y\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\left(x^{(2)}\mid y\right)</annotation></semantics></math> guarantees that agreement between the two views is more likely to reflect the true label rather than correlated errors. Hence, as the independence parameter increases, so does the quality of pseudo-labels, and thus</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\Gamma}{\partial\,\mathrm{indep}}\;&gt;\;0\;\text{(Monotonicity in conditional independence)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">∂</mo><mi>indep</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em">&gt;</mo><mrow><mn> 0</mn><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">(Monotonicity in conditional independence)</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\Gamma}{\partial\,\mathrm{indep}}\;&gt;\;0\;\text{(Monotonicity in conditional independence)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Since each partial derivative has the stated sign, the three equivalent monotonicity claims follow immediately. This completes the proof of Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 3.6 (Benefit of Unlabeled Data) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 3.7 (Generalization Bound) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a></h3>

<div id="A2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof B.4</span></span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">By standard VC/Rademacher arguments, with probability at least <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\frac{\delta}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\frac{\delta}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> over the draw of <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, every <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R(h)\leq\widehat{R}_{N_{L}}(h)+C_{1}\sqrt{\frac{d_{\mathrm{eff}}\ln\left(N_{L}/d_{\mathrm{eff}}\right)+\ln(2/\delta)}{N_{L}}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R(h)\leq\widehat{R}_{N_{L}}(h)+C_{1}\sqrt{\frac{d_{\mathrm{eff}}\ln\left(N_{L}/d_{\mathrm{eff}}\right)+\ln(2/\delta)}{N_{L}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here, <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\text{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">eff</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\text{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math> is either the VC dimension or a Rademacher-based measure of <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is an absolute constant. By design, our co-training algorithm enforces that <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> achieves empirical agreement on the unlabeled pool: it either pseudo-labels or directly penalizes discrepancies via <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{agree}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">agree</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{agree}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A(h)=\frac{1}{N_{U}}\sum_{j=1}^{N_{U}}1\left\{h^{(1)}\left(u_{j}\right)\neq h^{(2)}\left(u_{j}\right)\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></msubsup><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>{</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(h)=\frac{1}{N_{U}}\sum_{j=1}^{N_{U}}1\left\{h^{(1)}\left(u_{j}\right)\neq h^{(2)}\left(u_{j}\right)\right\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the empirical disagreement rate, and write <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> for its true counterpart. Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 3.6 (Benefit of Unlabeled Data) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a> shows that enforcing low <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(h)</annotation></semantics></math> under conditional independence yields a nonnegative benefit <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\left(\frac{N_{l}}{N_{l}+N_{U}},A(h),\text{indep}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">indep</mtext><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\left(\frac{N_{l}}{N_{l}+N_{U}},A(h),\text{indep}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> such that, on the event <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{A(h)\approx d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right)\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{A(h)\approx d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right)\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>, one can subtract <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> from the usual risk. Formally,</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R(h)\leq\widehat{R}_{N_{L}}(h)-\Gamma\left(\frac{N_{U}}{N_{L}+N_{U}},A(h),\text{indep}\right)+\Delta(h)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">indep</mtext><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R(h)\leq\widehat{R}_{N_{L}}(h)-\Gamma\left(\frac{N_{U}}{N_{L}+N_{U}},A(h),\text{indep}\right)+\Delta(h),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(h)</annotation></semantics></math> captures any slack from finite unlabeled-sample estimation of <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. By a Hoeffding bound on the <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> independent indicator variables in <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(h)</annotation></semantics></math>, with probability at least <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\frac{\delta}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\frac{\delta}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, uniformly over <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|A(h)-d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right)\right|\leq C_{2}\sqrt{\frac{\ln(2/\delta)}{N_{U}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mfrac></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|A(h)-d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right)\right|\leq C_{2}\sqrt{\frac{\ln(2/\delta)}{N_{U}}}</annotation></semantics></math> for some absolute constant <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, we can absorb <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(h)</annotation></semantics></math> into a single term <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}\sqrt{\frac{\ln(2/\delta)}{N_{L}+N_{U}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}\sqrt{\frac{\ln(2/\delta)}{N_{L}+N_{U}}}</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> both contribute to our overall confidence budget. Taking a union bound over the two <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\frac{\delta}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\frac{\delta}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> events, we get with probability <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math> that both Eq.(11) and (12) hold and that <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(h)</annotation></semantics></math> is close to <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting the concentration of <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(h)</annotation></semantics></math> into Eq.(12) and combining with <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A)</annotation></semantics></math> yields</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R(h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R(h)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\widehat{R}_{N_{L}}(h)+C_{1}\sqrt{\frac{d_{\text{eff}}\ln\left(N_{L}/d_{\text{eff}}\right)+\ln(2/\delta)}{N_{L}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">eff</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">eff</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\widehat{R}_{N_{L}}(h)+C_{1}\sqrt{\frac{d_{\text{eff}}\ln\left(N_{L}/d_{\text{eff}}\right)+\ln(2/\delta)}{N_{L}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\Gamma\left(\frac{N_{U}}{N_{L}+N_{U}},\text{agreement, indep}\right)+C_{2}\sqrt{\frac{\ln(2/\delta)}{N_{L}+N_{U}}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">agreement, indep</mtext><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\Gamma\left(\frac{N_{U}}{N_{L}+N_{U}},\text{agreement, indep}\right)+C_{2}\sqrt{\frac{\ln(2/\delta)}{N_{L}+N_{U}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Renaming constants and replacing <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ln(2/\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ln(2/\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ln(1/\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ln(1/\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> up to constant factors completes the stated bound. This completes the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 3.7 (Generalization Bound) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.5 </span>Proof of Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Corollary 3.8 (Effect of Unlabeled Sample Size) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a></h3>

<div id="A2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof B.5</span></span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recall the bound from Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 3.7 (Generalization Bound) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a> (Eq.(11) and (12)), and from Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 3.6 (Benefit of Unlabeled Data) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>, <math id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is increasing in the fraction <math id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{N_{U}}{N_{L}+N_{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{N_{U}}{N_{L}+N_{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> grows with <math id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}</annotation></semantics></math> fixed, this fraction increases monotonically, so <math id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> strictly increases. Further, the term <math id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}\sqrt{\frac{\ln(1/\delta)}{N_{L}+N_{U}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}\sqrt{\frac{\ln(1/\delta)}{N_{L}+N_{U}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a strictly decreasing function of the total sample size <math id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}+N_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}+N_{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, as <math id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> increases, this penalty shrinks. Combining these two monotonicities shows that the RHS of the generalization bound tightens as <math id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{U}</annotation></semantics></math> grows. This completes the proof of Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Corollary 3.8 (Effect of Unlabeled Sample Size) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.6 </span>Proof of Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem9" title="Corollary 3.9 (Effect of View Independence and Agreement) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.9</span></a></h3>

<div id="A2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof B.6</span></span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">From Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 3.7 (Generalization Bound) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a>, we have</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\left(h_{\theta}\right)\leq\widehat{R}_{N_{L}}\left(h_{\theta}\right)+C\left(N_{L},d_{\mathrm{eff}},\delta\right)-\Gamma\left(\eta,d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right),\text{indep}\right)+C^{\prime}\left(N_{L}+N_{U},\delta\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">indep</mtext><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>U</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\left(h_{\theta}\right)\leq\widehat{R}_{N_{L}}\left(h_{\theta}\right)+C\left(N_{L},d_{\mathrm{eff}},\delta\right)-\Gamma\left(\eta,d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right),\text{indep}\right)+C^{\prime}\left(N_{L}+N_{U},\delta\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here <math id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> by Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 3.6 (Benefit of Unlabeled Data) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>, and the two residual terms <math id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> do not depend on view-agreement or independence.
By Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 3.6 (Benefit of Unlabeled Data) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\Gamma}{\partial d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right)}&lt;0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\frac{\partial[-\Gamma]}{\partial d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right)}&gt;0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\Gamma}{\partial d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right)}&lt;0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\frac{\partial[-\Gamma]}{\partial d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right)}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Thus, as the disagreement <math id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\left(h^{(1)},h^{(2)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> decreases (i.e., agreement increases), <math id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> strictly decreases, making the RHS of the bound strictly smaller.
Similarly, Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 3.6 (Benefit of Unlabeled Data) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a> gives</span></p>
<table id="A2.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial\Gamma}{\partial\text{indep}}&gt;0\Longrightarrow\frac{\partial[-\Gamma]}{\partial\text{indep}}&lt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">indep</mtext></mrow></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">indep</mtext></mrow></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\Gamma}{\partial\text{indep}}&gt;0\Longrightarrow\frac{\partial[-\Gamma]}{\partial\text{indep}}&lt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hence, as the conditional-independence assumption strengthens (i.e., the factorization <math id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P\left(x^{(1)},x^{(2)}\mid\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">∣</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\left(x^{(1)},x^{(2)}\mid\right.</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y)=P\left(x^{(1)}\mid y\right)P\left(x^{(2)}\mid y\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">𝑂𝑃𝐸𝑁</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y)=P\left(x^{(1)}\mid y\right)P\left(x^{(2)}\mid y\right)</annotation></semantics></math> becomes tighter), <math id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> again decreases, tightening the bound.
Neither <math id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> nor <math id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on view-agreement or independence, so the only terms affected by these factors are <math id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. Since increased agreement and stronger independence both strictly increase <math id="A2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, they strictly decrease the RHS of the generalization bound. This completes the proof of Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem9" title="Corollary 3.9 (Effect of View Independence and Agreement) ‣ 3.3 Generalization Bound for Multimodal Co-Training ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ Efficient Generalization via Multimodal Co-Training under Data Scarcity and Distribution Shift" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.9</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
